reagent-name-methylxanthine = methylxanthine
reagent-desc-methylxanthine = A purified caffeine-like substance sometimes prescribed in small doses to counter attention deficiency. Overdose may result in hyperactivity, jittering, vomiting, and convulsions.
methylxanthine-effect-awake = You feel more awake.
methylxanthine-effect-focus = You feel like you're able to focus.
methylxanthine-effect-attentive = You feel attentive.

reagent-name-sertraline = sertraline
reagent-desc-sertraline = A potent anti-depressant used to counteract a wide range of depressive and anxiety disorders. Side effects may include appetite changes, nausea, and headache. Do not overdose.
sertraline-effect-calmer = You feel calmer, and more confident.
sertraline-effect-breathing = Your breathing is steadying.
sertraline-effect-feel-better = You feel a little better about yourself.
sertraline-effect-alright = Feels like it's going to be alright.
sertraline-effect-head-pain = Your head hurts.
sertraline-effect-nausea = You feel nauseous...
sertraline-effect-head-pound = Your head is pounding!

reagent-name-valproate = valproate
reagent-desc-valproate = An antiepileptic chemical that is also frequently used for mood stabilisation and anti-manic treatment. Side effects may include nausea, weakness, and dizziness. Do not overdose.
valproate-effect-calmer = You feel calmer.
valproate-effect-less-overwhelming = Everything feels less overwhelming.
valproate-effect-tired = You feel tired...

reagent-name-lurasidone = lurasidone
reagent-desc-lurasidone = A powerful anti-psychotic derived from an advanced compound commonly found in psicodine. Often used to treat patients exhibiting risks to self or others.